Below is a “road‑map” showing  

1. which columns in the three tables are needed and why, and  
2. ten exploratory‑to‑final SQL statements (each annotated) that gradually build the answer.

Relevant columns and their purpose
• `condition.plot_sequence_number` – join key to population.  
• `condition.inventory_year` – analysis year (2015–2017).  
• `condition.state_code`, `condition.state_code_name` – identify state.  
• `condition.condition_status_code` – keep only live forest conditions (= 1).  
• `condition.proportion_basis` – tells whether a record is SUBP or MACR.  
• `condition.condition_proportion_unadjusted` – P in the area formula.  

• `population.plot_sequence_number` – join key to condition.  
• `population.inventory_year` – match to condition year.  
• `population.evaluation_type` – filter to ‘EXPCURR’.  
• `population.expansion_factor` – E in the area formula.  
• `population.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot` – As.  
• `population.adjustment_factor_for_the_macroplot` – Am.  
• `population.state_code` – cross‑check state if desired.

With these columns we can compute

      Subplot_Area = E * P * As   when proportion_basis = 'SUBP'
      Macroplot_Area = E * P * Am  when proportion_basis = 'MACR'

Ten progressively more complex queries
```sql
-- 1) What evaluation types exist in the population table?
SELECT DISTINCT evaluation_type
FROM `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population`
LIMIT 20;

-- 2) Confirm that years 2015‑2017 are present in the population table
SELECT DISTINCT inventory_year
FROM `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population`
WHERE inventory_year BETWEEN 2015 AND 2017
ORDER BY inventory_year;

-- 3) What proportion_basis codes exist in the condition table?
SELECT DISTINCT proportion_basis
FROM `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`
LIMIT 20;

-- 4) Peek at condition records for 2015 with live forest status
SELECT plot_sequence_number, inventory_year, state_code,
       condition_proportion_unadjusted, proportion_basis
FROM `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`
WHERE inventory_year = 2015
  AND condition_status_code = 1
LIMIT 20;

-- 5) Check typical adjustment factors in the population table
SELECT adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot     AS As,
       adjustment_factor_for_the_macroplot   AS Am,
       COUNT(*) AS n
FROM `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population`
WHERE evaluation_type = 'EXPCURR'
  AND inventory_year BETWEEN 2015 AND 2017
GROUP BY As, Am
ORDER BY n DESC
LIMIT 20;

-- 6) Join one small sample (Oregon‑2016) and compute subplot areas
SELECT c.plot_sequence_number,
       p.expansion_factor * c.condition_proportion_unadjusted *
       p.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot AS subplot_area
FROM `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`  AS c
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population` AS p
  ON  p.plot_sequence_number = c.plot_sequence_number
  AND p.inventory_year      = c.inventory_year
WHERE c.inventory_year = 2016
  AND c.state_code     = 41      -- Oregon
  AND c.condition_status_code = 1
  AND p.evaluation_type = 'EXPCURR'
  AND c.proportion_basis = 'SUBP'
LIMIT 20;

-- 7) Average subplot area by state & year, 2015‑17
SELECT c.inventory_year,
       c.state_code,
       AVG(p.expansion_factor * c.condition_proportion_unadjusted *
           p.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot) AS avg_subplot_area
FROM `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`  AS c
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population` AS p
  ON  p.plot_sequence_number = c.plot_sequence_number
  AND p.inventory_year      = c.inventory_year
WHERE c.condition_status_code = 1
  AND p.evaluation_type      = 'EXPCURR'
  AND c.proportion_basis     = 'SUBP'
  AND c.inventory_year BETWEEN 2015 AND 2017
GROUP BY c.inventory_year, c.state_code
LIMIT 20;

-- 8) Average macroplot area by state & year, 2015‑17
SELECT c.inventory_year,
       c.state_code,
       AVG(p.expansion_factor * c.condition_proportion_unadjusted *
           p.adjustment_factor_for_the_macroplot) AS avg_macro_area
FROM `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`  AS c
JOIN `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population` AS p
  ON  p.plot_sequence_number = c.plot_sequence_number
  AND p.inventory_year      = c.inventory_year
WHERE c.condition_status_code = 1
  AND p.evaluation_type      = 'EXPCURR'
  AND c.proportion_basis     = 'MACR'
  AND c.inventory_year BETWEEN 2015 AND 2017
GROUP BY c.inventory_year, c.state_code
LIMIT 20;

-- 9) Rank subplot averages and pick the largest state per year
WITH sub AS (
  SELECT c.inventory_year AS yr,
         c.state_code     AS st,
         AVG(p.expansion_factor * c.condition_proportion_unadjusted *
             p.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot) AS avg_area
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`  AS c
  JOIN `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population` AS p
    ON p.plot_sequence_number = c.plot_sequence_number
   AND p.inventory_year      = c.inventory_year
  WHERE c.condition_status_code = 1
    AND p.evaluation_type      = 'EXPCURR'
    AND c.proportion_basis     = 'SUBP'
    AND c.inventory_year BETWEEN 2015 AND 2017
  GROUP BY yr, st)
SELECT 'Subplot'            AS plot_type,
       yr                   AS year,
       st                   AS state_code,
       avg_area
FROM  (SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY yr ORDER BY avg_area DESC) AS rn
       FROM sub)
WHERE rn = 1
ORDER BY year;

--10) FINAL ANSWER: union subplot & macroplot winners 2015‑2017
WITH
subplot AS (
  SELECT 'Subplot' AS plot_type, yr, st, avg_area
  FROM (
       SELECT c.inventory_year AS yr,
              c.state_code     AS st,
              AVG(p.expansion_factor * c.condition_proportion_unadjusted *
                  p.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot) AS avg_area,
              ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY c.inventory_year
                                 ORDER BY AVG(p.expansion_factor * c.condition_proportion_unadjusted *
                                              p.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot) DESC) AS rk
       FROM `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`  AS c
       JOIN `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population` AS p
         ON p.plot_sequence_number = c.plot_sequence_number
        AND p.inventory_year      = c.inventory_year
       WHERE c.condition_status_code = 1
         AND p.evaluation_type      = 'EXPCURR'
         AND c.proportion_basis     = 'SUBP'
         AND c.inventory_year BETWEEN 2015 AND 2017
       GROUP BY yr, st )
  WHERE rk = 1 ),
macroplot AS (
  SELECT 'Macroplot' AS plot_type, yr, st, avg_area
  FROM (
       SELECT c.inventory_year AS yr,
              c.state_code     AS st,
              AVG(p.expansion_factor * c.condition_proportion_unadjusted *
                  p.adjustment_factor_for_the_macroplot) AS avg_area,
              ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY c.inventory_year
                                 ORDER BY AVG(p.expansion_factor * c.condition_proportion_unadjusted *
                                              p.adjustment_factor_for_the_macroplot) DESC) AS rk
       FROM `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`  AS c
       JOIN `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population` AS p
         ON p.plot_sequence_number = c.plot_sequence_number
        AND p.inventory_year      = c.inventory_year
       WHERE c.condition_status_code = 1
         AND p.evaluation_type      = 'EXPCURR'
         AND c.proportion_basis     = 'MACR'
         AND c.inventory_year BETWEEN 2015 AND 2017
       GROUP BY yr, st )
  WHERE rk = 1 )
SELECT plot_type, yr AS year, st AS state_code, avg_area
FROM (SELECT * FROM subplot UNION ALL SELECT * FROM macroplot)
ORDER BY year, plot_type;
```

Running the last query will return six rows – the state with the highest average **subplot** area and the state with the highest average **macroplot** area for each of the years 2015, 2016, 2017 – together with the computed average sizes.
